est files, test-case registries and declared tests roots produced by supported workflows; non-Python fixture text, non-default test-collection patterns, hand-chosen test-case aliases and tests roots reached through a symlink are outside it.\"","+detail = \"A test-file back-link declaration names test cases, and each named id must be a live test case. In a Python test file, declarations in the module docstring and in a test's docstring are read; in another supported test-file type, declarations are read at file level. A byte-order mark at the start of a file does not change its reading. The two Python levels union. A test repeating its module's test case is no finding. That reading judges the declarations alone: their equality with the Evidence cell is a separate rule. A non-docstring string literal, a backtick-quoted token and an unsupported test-file type make no declaration. A declaration outside a read position, including a pytest-fixture docstring, is refused as misplaced. The following are refused as malformed: a declaration whose list is not test-case ids only; a read-position line whose token follows prose and names a test case; and a next line that continues a valid list. The after-prose refusal directs the declaration to the start of its own line. An id absent from the live test-case registry, including an example or retired id, is refused as unknown; a Drafted test case is live. A test file that cannot be decoded or parsed is refused as unreadable. Every live id in a valid declaration maps to its test node. Each refusal names the repository-relative file, line and shape. Reading test files does not change source-file declaration reading. An absent tests root, or an absent test-case registry, makes the check vacuous. Findings warn without strict checking and error under strict checking; they gate from DevStg-Impl and remain warnings below that rung, including in a newly created project. The contract covers test files, test-case registries and declared tests roots produced by supported workflows; non-Python fixture text, non-default test-collection patterns, hand-chosen test-case aliases and tests roots reached through a symlink are outside it.\""," rationale = \"A tag that is silently misplaced, malformed, dangling or unreadable can make a test case appear absent without an attributable finding. Keeping the declaration grammar separate from source-file reading prevents this added input from changing established source analysis.\"","-test_refs = \"TC-404; TC-405; TC-406; TC-407; TC-408; TC-409\"","+test_refs = \"TC-404; TC-405; TC-406; TC-407; TC-408; TC-409; TC-411\""," status = \"Approved\""," component = \"CMP-006\""," phase = 6"," ","+[design.LLR-366]","+sr_refs = [\"SR-157\"]","+title = \"Test-case evidence and test-file declarations agree\"","+module = \"project-trajectory/scripts/trajectory_arch.py\"","+code_symbol = \"tc_tag_findings/_tag_evidence_findings\"","+detail = \"For each live test case, including a Drafted case, the declarations in test files under the declared tests root equal the parts of its Evidence cell. The cell is read as its other readers read it, so separator choice changes nothing and a parenthetical note supplies no part. A part is its file, or its file and test with a class retained and a parametrized case removed. A file part binds only to a file-level declaration and a test part only to that test's declaration, so a test part in a file read at file level is two differences. A test's declaration repeating its file's declaration is still a declaration, so its cell names that test as well. A part outside the tests root is outside the rule. A tests root of `.` holds every part in the repository. An uncovered part is reported as evidence without a tag, and an unreferenced declaration as tag without evidence; each names the test case and node. A file refused as unreadable adds no finding here, and an absent tests root or test-case registry makes the check vacuous. Findings warn without strict 